// Source-body reconciliation. Timings and layouts are report-conditioned;
// the completion example is synthetic, not a measured cluster result.
export function rlInfrastructurePreservation({p, eq, table, cite, related}){return {
verifier:p("A verifier must canonicalize harmless answer differences without accepting different meanings. Parser ambiguities, hard-coded visible tests and malformed outputs with default success scores are concrete failure cases. Audit the checker before increasing rollout volume.")+table("Feedback quality is a spectrum, not an optimizer choice", ["Feedback", "Useful check", "Remaining risk"], [["Exact or symbolic answer", "Equivalent result under declared assumptions", "Parser errors or incomplete equivalence rules"], ["Code/game/environment outcome", "Private tests or required final state", "Visible-test shortcuts or simulator exploits"], ["Reference or rubric-based judge", "Calibrated scores and independent inspection", "Subjective misspecification and correlated judge errors"]])+p("A rubric-based AI judge can cover research synthesis, ambiguous planning or creative work. It is not automatically an exact verifier. Softer feedback inherits evaluator misspecification; several judges can still share the same error. Budget submissions and keep hidden checks inaccessible through tools. Public checks should give diagnostic feedback without exposing the complete private grader."),
pipeline:table("Ways to initialize and alternate verifiable-reward training", ["Pipeline", "What it provides", "What still needs evaluation"], [["Base model → RLVR", "Exploration without an initial reasoning-SFT stage", "Readability, mixed languages and success frequency"], ["SFT → RLVR", "Response format and demonstrated strategies", "Whether outcome training improves held-out behavior"], ["Distillation → RLVR", "Teacher traces before student-generated attempts", "Student-specific mistakes and reachable successes"], ["Repeated stages", "Reasoning SFT, RLVR, rejection/distillation, general and agentic alignment", "Retention and attribution across stages"]])+p("Outcome feedback leaves the trace unspecified. Decomposition, backtracking, intermediate checks, self-written tests and adaptive stopping may emerge or become more frequent. Their appearance does not prove that the base model had no related behavior. These pipelines are alternatives, not a universal ranking.")+p("Increase rollout counts or generation budgets, mix easier tasks, or initialize from useful demonstrations when successful contrast is too rare. Do not maximize difficulty for its own sake. All-equal group filtering applies to centered group objectives, not every possible RL update. Penalizing length before reliable success can suppress exploration. Recheck knowledge, instruction following and open-domain usefulness after specialized math/code stages."),
async:eq(String.raw`\begin{aligned}T_{\mathrm{sync}}&\approx\max_i T_i\\\frac{c}{NK}&\ge\lambda, \quad j=\lceil\lambda NK\rceil\\T_{\mathrm{partial}}&\approx T_{(j)}\end{aligned}`, "N prompts, K attempts per prompt, c completed attempts;0&lt;λ≤1. T(j) is the j-th ordered duration. These collector estimates exclude grading, transfer and learner work.")+table("Synthetic collector example:two prompts×two attempts, λ=0.75", ["Sorted completion time", "Completed attempts", "Collector threshold"], [["1 s", "1 of 4", "Wait"], ["2 s", "2 of 4", "Wait"], ["3 s", "3 of 4", "75% reached"], ["9 s", "4 of 4", "Synchronous tail finishes"]])+p("This example changes the collection threshold from 9 s to 3 s, not total training time. Unfinished attempts keep their trajectory state and resume later. A GRPO learner must still respect same-prompt group membership and an explicit incomplete-group policy. It must not combine unrelated prompts merely because their attempts finished together.")+p("Generation can consume more hardware than learning when traces are long or each prompt needs many candidates. Tool latency adds another resource bottleneck. Measure rollout, learner, grading and transfer costs rather than assuming inference dominates every run."),
environment:p("AgentENV uses incremental dirty-page checkpoints to suspend mutable microVM state, release runtime resources and restore before a later tool action. The reported 133 ms checkpoint / 49 ms resume values do not include every workload's complete recovery cost.")+`<div class="dense-flow" role="img" aria-label="Final task state forks into a preserved agent state and a separate judge state. Only the judge copy receives verification side effects."><div>Final task state</div><span>→ fork →</span><div>Agent copy<br><small>preserved</small></div><span>+</span><div>Judge copy<br><small>verification side effects</small></div></div>`+p("Separate judge forks allow independent checks and reproducible failure inspection. Grading must not mutate the agent's authoritative final state."),
cache:table("K3's reported tiered state placement", ["State", "Placement or transition", "Correctness condition"], [["Active attention prefix", "GPU cache", "Do not evict useful active state merely to create a copy"], ["Evicted idle prefixes", "CPU DRAM write-back pool", "Restore the matching prefix and version metadata"], ["Hybrid reusable prefix", "Attention blocks plus every recurrent checkpoint", "Sparse recurrent checkpoints restrict reusable boundaries"], ["Training weights/optimizer at rollout transition", "Offload under the reported NVMe budget", "Count transition and restore time"], ["Reference/teacher weights", "CPU;teacher chunks stream through reusable GPU buffers", "Preserve the declared scoring model and execution contract"]])+p("The report describes colocating phases on a few hundred GPUs. This saves a permanently separate fleet but adds offload and phase-transition costs. Only evicted idle prefixes need write-back; copying active state unnecessarily adds traffic. Compare total useful accelerator time, not each phase's isolated peak."),
recovery:p("Weight refresh can require resharding, layout conversion, dtype conversion and replication management. Sparse refreshes increase policy age; frequent refreshes consume transfer, reload or requantization time. Tune cadence alongside the update recipe and inspect reward/completion/ratio tails by staleness bucket.")+p("Learner and rollout precision need not be bitwise identical. Measure the mismatch, keep its scoring contract stable, and validate any engine or precision transition. Preserve tokenization, assistant rendering, tool-call formatting and turn boundaries; protocol changes can alter a multi-turn task.")+p("Use replica and progress watchdogs, liveness checks and router-level circuit breaking. An isolated inference replica failure should reduce capacity rather than terminate the whole job. Recovery must preserve sample identity to avoid duplicate accepted updates.")+p("Throttle admission when active/queued requests, GPU KV use, CPU prefix use or environment capacity exceed the budget. Raise it only when queues and memory permit. Report restore latency, failed grading and reward/completion rates by policy age, not just average throughput."),
sources:p(cite("https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/the-state-of-llm-reasoning-model-training", "Raschka:reasoning-model training survey")+" · "+cite("https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12948", "DeepSeek-R1")+" · "+cite("https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.03300", "DeepSeekMath")+" · "+cite("https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12599", "Kimi k1.5")+" · "+cite("https://github.com/MoonshotAI/Kimi-K3/blob/main/k3_tech_report.pdf", "Kimi K3 infrastructure"))+p(related("post-training/agents", "Agent tasks and environment construction")+" · "+related("execution/serving", "Serving and prefix reuse"))
};}
